% ch-05-ex-02-built-in-types.glp
%
% This file presents the §5.2 universal List type from book p 48 (PDF p 60),
% byte-exact from the PDF. The book's §5.2 prose introduces three built-in
% types — Number, Any, Atom — that are pre-existing GLP primitives (no code
% declaration needed). The List definition uses Any to make a universal list.
%
% Programs included (byte-exact):
%   §5.2 (book p 48): List ::= [] ; [Any | List].  — universal list via built-in Any
%
% A minimal recogniser is_list/1 is added below the type def so the learner
% can run goals against the universal-list type. Compare its acceptance with
% ex-01's is_numlist/1 to see how Any (universal) differs from Number (typed).

%% List type: a universal list. Either the empty list [], or [Head | Tail]
%% where Head is of the built-in type Any (any term) and Tail is again a List.
%% Compare with §5.1.3's NumList — only the element type changes (Number → Any).
List ::= [] ; [Any | List].

%% --- TYPE RECOGNISER (probe procedure so goals can be run on List) ---

%% is_list/1: succeeds when its argument is a List value (universal — any term
%% may be a list element).
procedure is_list(List?).
%% Base case: [] is a List.
is_list([]).
%% Recursive case: [_|Rest] is a List if Rest is a List. The head element
%% is unconstrained because Any accepts any term.
is_list([_|Rest]) :- is_list(Rest?).
